sect1.para:
📍 Location
After contentDisposition.

🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
Whenever someone asks for "a page of the app" — the lobby, or some deep hallway whose exact room we don't need to check — the receptionist hands them the same magic brochure (index.html). Inside the brochure, a little guide (the SPA router) looks at where they wanted to go and walks them there without ever leaving the brochure.

⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
Reads index.html from disk on every request (no read-through cache: serveStatic gives .html Cache-Control: no-cache, and this handler sends no cache headers at all, so the browser must revalidate — an intentional trade of a little latency for instant deploy propagation). Error path answers 500 with an operator-focused message ("place it next to
   server.js"). Success path stamps securityHeaders(res) then writes 200 with text/html; charset=utf-8.

📥 Inputs


File: <project root>/index.html — the SPA shell documented in dox/main/index.dox.



📤 Outputs
The full HTML shell; the browser then loads, in order, the CSS chain (tokens.css → base.css → components.css → home.css → browse.css → performance-modes.css) and the deferred JS chain (resource-service.js → performance-monitor.js → theme.js → i18n.js → autocomplete.js → home.js → browse.js → router.js).

🔗 Trace Upward
Invoked from two places: the explicit SPA branch (/, /index.html, /browse, /browse/... — after its extension whitelist check) and serveStatic's fallback for missing .html/extensionless files.

🔗 Trace Downward
Depends on fs.readFile, path.join, securityHeaders, sendText, and the presence of index.html on disk.

🤝 Trace Sideways
This is the Node twin of nginx's @spa location in nginx.conf (rewrite ^ /index.html last;): both exist so that deep links like /browse/ISOs/ survive refresh. In production nginx usually answers first; this handler covers npm start development and any Node-served deployment. The consumer of the shell is assets/js/router.js, whose resolveRoute() understands exactly the same route vocabulary (/, /index.html → home; /browse, /browse/... → browse).

💻 Actual Code
functionserveSPA(res){
constindexPath=path.join(ROOT,"index.html");
fs.readFile(indexPath,(err,data)=>{
if(err){
sendText(res,500,"index.htmlmissing—placeitnexttoserver.js");
return;
}
securityHeaders(res);
res.writeHead(200,{"Content-Type":"text/html;charset=utf-8"});
res.end(data);
});
}


⚠️ Breaking Change Warning Renaming or moving index.html breaks every SPA deep link and the error message becomes a lie. Serving a different shell (e.g. the legacy browse.html) would strand the deferred script chain in index.html (theme.js, performance-monitor.js, autocomplete.js are loaded only there) and silently disable theming, perf modes and search suggestions. Also note the response carries no explicit cache header here — if you add caching, coordinate with serveStatic's no-cache policy for .html.

📍 Location
After serveSPA.

🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
The supply closet attendant. Every picture, stylesheet, script and font the brochure mentions lives in this closet. You ask by name, the attendant checks you're not asking for something outside the closet (no peeking into the office!), finds the box, sticks the right label on it, and hands it over. If a picture is missing, they hand you a friendly little "picture
   not found" doodle instead of an error page. And if you ask for something that isn't a closet item at all — you get the magic brochure again.

⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
Four phases, in order:

Path sanitisation — path.normalize(reqPath).replace(/^(\.\.[\/\\])+/, "") collapses dot-segments and strips leading parent-jumps; path.join(ROOT, …) maps / and "" to index.html; the extension is lowercased once.

Traversal guard — isInsideRoot(ROOT, fp) plus an explicit re-admission of index.html; violations get 403 Forbidden via sendText.

Contentful fallbacks (inside fs.readFile's error callback): missing images (png/jpg/jpeg/webp/ico/svg) get an inline 64×64 SVG placeholder (a 200 response with image/svg+xml!); missing pages (.html or no extension) get serveSPA(res); anything else gets 404 — ${reqPath}.

Success — securityHeaders(res), then writeHead(200) with the MIME from MIMES (fallback application/octet-stream) and a cache policy: no-cache for .html, public, max-age=3600 (one hour) otherwise.



📥 Inputs


reqPath — the decoded pathname (e.g. /assets/css/base.css).

Files on disk under the project root: assets/**, fonts, favicon.



📤 Outputs
Static bytes + headers, or one of the three fallbacks above.

🔗 Trace Upward
Called from the main router's final serveStatic(pathname, res) branch and from the SPA branch when the path has a known non-HTML extension. The consumers are the <link>/<img>/<script> tags of index.html (tokens.css, base.css, components.css, home.css, browse.css, performance-modes.css, /assets/icon/logo.webp, /assets/icon/files-logo.webp, all eight deferred scripts) and the runtime-injected markup of home.js/browse.js/autocomplete.js.

🔗 Trace Downward
Depends on path, fs, isInsideRoot, MIMES, securityHeaders, sendText, serveSPA, and the inline SVG string constant embedded below.

🤝 Trace Sideways
The inline-SVG-on-404 trick is why the navbar logo carries an inline onerror="this.style.display='none'" in index.html — the frontend has its own missing-image fallback, and the two coexist (server fallback for <img> loads that never reach JS handlers, onerror for robustness). nginx implements the same "serve assets with long cache" concept via its regex location (30d, immutable) — production caches longer than dev.

💻 Actual Code
functionserveStatic(reqPath,res){
constsafe=path.normalize(reqPath).replace(/^(\.\.[\/\\])+/,"");
constfp=path.join(ROOT,safe==="/"||safe===""?"index.html":safe);
constext=path.extname(fp).toLowerCase();

if(!isInsideRoot(ROOT,fp)&&path.resolve(fp)!==path.resolve(ROOT,"index.html")){
sendText(res,403,"Forbidden");
return;
}

fs.readFile(fp,(err,data)=>{
if(err){
if([".png",".jpg",".jpeg",".webp",".ico",".svg"].includes(ext)){
constsvg=`<svgxmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"width="64"height="64"viewBox="002424"fill="none"stroke="#64748b"stroke-width="1.5"><rectx="3"y="3"width="18"height="18"rx="3"opacity="0.3"/><circlecx="9"cy="9"r="2"/><pathd="m2115-3.086-3.086a22000-2.8280L621"/></svg>`;
res.writeHead(200,{"Content-Type":"image/svg+xml"});
res.end(svg);
return;
}

if(!ext||ext===".html"){
serveSPA(res);
return;
}

sendText(res,404,`404—${reqPath}`);
return;
}

securityHeaders(res);
res.writeHead(200,{
"Content-Type":MIMES[ext]||"application/octet-stream",
"Cache-Control":ext===".html"?"no-cache":"public,max-age=3600",
});
res.end(data);
});
}


⚠️ Breaking Change Warning This is the file-serving heart of the app. Softening the traversal guard exposes the source tree (including lib/ and server.js if the router blocklist is ever bypassed). Removing the SVG placeholder changes how the UI looks under broken image URLs (home/browse cards would show browser broken-image icons). Changing the one-hour max-age invalidates the frontend's assumptions about asset freshness during deploys. Note the SVG fallback deliberately answers 200 — that is what keeps <img> elements rendering; changing it to 404 must be coordinated with the onerror handler on the logo in index.html.

📍 Location
After serveStatic.

🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
The big-box counter. Small answers go in envelopes; a 5-gigabyte disk image goes into a stream — the receptionist starts rolling the box out the door immediately, piece by piece, instead of trying to lift the whole thing at once. And if the customer hangs up halfway, they stop rolling immediately too.

⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth)
Builds headers from fileInfo (produced by filesService.getDownloadTarget, which has already validated existence and file-ness): MIME from MIMES (fallback octet-stream), exact Content-Length from stat, the RFC 5987 disposition from contentDisposition, Cache-Control: private, max-age=0, must-revalidate, permissive CORS, and X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff. Three lifecycle details make it production-grade:

HEAD support — headers only, res.end() with no body.

Security headers before streaming — securityHeaders(res) then writeHead.

Backpressure & cancellation — fs.createReadStream(...).pipe(res) honours TCP backpressure; stream.on("error") answers 500 only if headers have not been sent yet (!res.headersSent), otherwise it res.destroy()s mid-flight; req.on("close") destroys the stream so an aborted download stops disk reads.



📥 Inputs


req — used for method (HEAD) and the close (abort) event.

fileInfo — { absPath, fileName, size, mtime } from lib/files-service.js.



📤 Outputs
A streamed file with Content-Disposition: attachment; ..., or a 500 text response on early read failure.

🔗 Trace Upward
Triggered exclusively by the /api/download router branch. Frontend producers of those URLs: ResourceService.downloadHref() used by home.js (quick-links, search results, release cards), browse.js (file rows), and autocomplete.js (itemHref for non-directory suggestions). Because the SPA never fetches this URL with fetch(), the browser's native download manager is the real consumer — which is why router.js's click interceptor explicitly skips file-extension links.

🔗 Trace Downward
Depends on fs.createReadStream, path.extname, MIMES, contentDisposition, securityHeaders, sendText.

🤝 Trace Sideways
The no-store on JSON endpoints vs private, must-revalidate on downloads mirrors the frontend cache split: resource-service.js caches metadata in sessionStorage for 5 minutes but never caches bytes. nginx.conf proxies this endpoint with proxy_buffering off and 3600s read/send timeouts — the numbers match exactly so a slow multi-GB download is never truncated by the proxy.

💻 Actual Code
functionstreamDownload(req,res,fileInfo){
constext=path.extname(fileInfo.fileName).toLowerCase();
constheaders={
"Content-Type":MIMES[ext]||"application/octet-stream",
"Content-Length":fileInfo.size,
"Content-Disposition":contentDisposition(fileInfo.fileName),
"Cache-Control":"private,max-age=0,must-revalidate",
"Access-Control-Allow-Origin":"*",
"X-Content-Type-Options":"nosniff",
};

if(req.method==="HEAD"){
res.writeHead(200,headers);
res.end();
return;
}

securityHeaders(res);
res.writeHead(200,headers);
conststream=fs.createReadStream(fileInfo.absPath);
stream.on("error",()=>{
if(!res.headersSent)sendText(res,500,"Failedtoreadfile");
elseres.destroy();
});
req.on("close",()=>stream.destroy());
stream.pipe(res);
}


⚠️ Breaking Change Warning The fileInfo shape (absPath, fileName, size) is a contract with lib/files-service.js's getDownloadTarget. Switching pipe(res) to a manual data/end loop without backpressure can balloon memory on large .iso/.qcow2/.vmdk files — the exact artefacts this portal exists to serve. Removing the req.on("close") handler makes aborted downloads keep reading disk. Removing res.headersSent checking throws ERR_HTTP_HEADERS_SENT on mid-stream failures. And per nginx.conf, the 3600s proxy timeouts assume downloads can legitimately run for an hour; adding buffering (res.end(buffer)) or changing cache headers interacts with that configuration.

📍 Location
After streamDownload; the largest single block in the file.

🎯 Purpose (ELI5)
This is the receptionist's actual brain — the big checklist they run through for every single visitor, in the same order every time: understand the request → handle special "may I pass?" pings → reject wrong methods → refuse dangerous doors → answer API questions → hand out pages → hand out closet items → and if anything explodes anywhere along the way, apologise politely in JSON.

⚙️ How It Works (Senior Depth) — Sub-blocks in exact order


🧩 1. Latency timer and URL parsing
Records const start = Date.now() for every log line, then parses req.url into a URL (against the synthetic http://localhost:${PORT} base) and percent-decodes url.pathname into pathname. Any parse or decode failure (URIError from malformed % sequences) lands in the catch with sendText(res, 400, "Bad Request").

🧩 2. CORS preflight (OPTIONS)
Any OPTIONS request gets 204 with Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *, Access-Control-Allow-Methods: GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Content-Type, Access-Control-Max-Age: 86400 (browsers cache the preflight for a day). This is what lets the portal be embedded or driven from other origins even though nginx only proxies /api/.

🧩 3. Method gate
Anything that is not GET or HEAD receives 405 Method Not Allowed before any routing happens — a quiet rejection of POST/PUT/DELETE scanners.

🧩 4. Security blocklist
Three prefixes are refused with 403 Forbidden + a log line: /.env (secrets!), /lib/ (server-side source: files-service.js, load-env.js), /node_modules/ (dependency source). This is the Node-side twin of nginx's location ~ /\. deny rule — both layers must stay because dev traffic skips nginx and production traffic skips neither.

🧩 5. /api/health + /__health
Calls the synchronous filesService.peekRootSync(PROJECT_ROOT) (safe in a hot path: one existsSync/statSync, no awaits) and answers with { status: "ok"|"degraded", service: "CloudAvid File Portal", uptime: process.uptime(), timestamp, filesRootConfigured, filesRootReady }. "ok" requires configured ∧ exists ∧ isDirectory; anything else is "degraded" (HTTP 200 either way, so uptime monitors alert on payload semantics, not status codes).

🧩 6. /api/categories
await filesService.listCategories(PROJECT_ROOT) → array of category objects (name, path, type: "directory", fileCount, folderCount, mtime, latestModified), sorted by name. Logged with the category count and latency.

🧩 7. /api/releases
Reads ?limit= (string or null; the service coerces and caps it), calls listLatestReleases(PROJECT_ROOT, limit) → the newest files across all categories with { name, path, category, type, size, mtime }.

🧩 8. /api/search
Reads ?q= (default "") and ?limit=, calls searchEntries(PROJECT_ROOT, q, { limit }) → { results, scanned, truncated }. Consumers: autocomplete.js (limit 6, debounced) and home.js (full search view).

🧩 9. /api/files and /api/files/...
Dual grammar: bare /api/files takes ?path= from the query string; /api/files/<rel> takes the tail via relFromPrefix. Empty rel lists the category roots. Consumer: ResourceService.listFiles() → browse.js.

🧩 10. /api/download and /api/download/...
Same dual grammar; resolves via getDownloadTarget(PROJECT_ROOT, rel) (which throws typed service errors for traversal/404/not-a-file — caught by the outer catch) and hands the descriptor to streamDownload. This is the only branch that streams bytes instead of JSON.

🧩 11. SPA routes
/, /index.html, /browse, and anything under /browse/ route to the app. A small wrinkle: if the path carries a known non-HTML extension (ext && MIMES[ext] && ext !== ".html"), it is served by serveStatic instead (logged with static), so /browse/logo.svg is an asset while /browse/ISOs/ is a client route. Everything else becomes serveSPA (logged with SPA).

🧩 12. Static fallback
Any other path goes to serveStatic(pathname, res) (logged with asset). Combined with the SPA branch this makes the server's page behaviour identical to nginx's try_files $uri $uri/ @spa.

🧩 13. The error net
The whole routing chain sits in one try: any thrown service error maps through sendServiceError (status from err.httpStatus, payload {error, code}) and is logged with err.code || err.message and latency.

📥 Inputs


req.url (path + query), req.method.

process.env.FILES_ROOT (read inside the service).

The on-disk downloads tree pointed at by FILES_ROOT.



📤 Outputs
JSON for the five read endpoints; streamed bytes for downloads; HTML shell for SPA routes; static bytes for assets; typed errors for everything that fails.

🔗 Trace Upward — who calls these endpoints, file by file


assets/js/services/resource-service.js — the single frontend API client: fetch("/api/categories") in getResources(), fetch("/api/files/" + …) in listFiles(), fetch("/api/search?" + params) in search(), fetch("/api/releases?limit=" + …) in getReleases(), and downloadHref() building /api/download/… links. It adds its own 5-minute sessionStorage cache (cav_resources_cache) on top of the no-store responses.

assets/js/home.js — getResources (category grid), getReleases(8) (latest releases), ResourceService.search(q, …) (search view), downloadHref/browseHref for links.

assets/js/browse.js — listFiles(relPath) per folder, downloadHref for file rows, browseHref for folder navigation.

assets/js/autocomplete.js — debounced ResourceService.search(q, {limit: 6}), Router.navigate for folders, downloadHref for files.

nginx.conf — proxies location /api/ → http://127.0.0.1:3000 with proxy_buffering off and 3600s timeouts; injects Cache-Control: no-store... always on API responses.

External uptime monitors — /api/health (or /__health).



🔗 Trace Downward — what this handler depends on


lib/files-service.js: peekRootSync, listCategories, listLatestReleases, searchEntries, listPath, getDownloadTarget — every one of the six functions of its public API.

This file's own helpers: sendText, sendJSON, sendServiceError, streamDownload, serveSPA, serveStatic, relFromPrefix, log.



🤝 Trace Sideways
The route vocabulary here (/, /browse/...) is mirrored by assets/js/router.js's resolveRoute() — the two files form the server/client routing contract that nginx.conf's @spa fallback completes. The dual grammar of /api/files and /api/download (?path= query or path segments) is a server-side affordance: today's frontend producers (verified across assets/js/) use only the segment form via encodeRelPath()/downloadHref()/listFiles(); the ?path= form is reserved surface for tools, scripts and curl workflows.

💻 Actual Code
constserver=http.createServer(async(req,res)=>{
conststart=Date.now();
letpathname;
leturl;

try{
url=newURL(req.url,`http://localhost:${PORT}`);
pathname=decodeURIComponent(url.pathname);
}catch{
sendText(res,400,"BadRequest");
return;
}

if(req.method==="OPTIONS"){
res.writeHead(204,{
"Access-Control-Allow-Origin":"*",
"Access-Control-Allow-Methods":"GET,HEAD,OPTIONS",
"Access-Control-Allow-Headers":"Content-Type",
"Access-Control-Max-Age":"86400",
});
res.end();
return;
}

if(req.method!=="GET"&&req.method!=="HEAD"){
sendText(res,405,"MethodNotAllowed");
return;
}

constblocked=pathname==="/.env"||pathname.startsWith("/lib/")||pathname.startsWith("/node_modules/");
if(blocked){
sendText(res,403,"Forbidden");
log(req.method,403,pathname);
return;
}

try{
if(pathname==="/__health"||pathname==="/api/health"){
constpeek=filesService.peekRootSync(PROJECT_ROOT);
sendJSON(res,200,{
status:peek.configured&&peek.exists&&peek.isDirectory?"ok":"degraded",
service:"CloudAvidFilePortal",
uptime:process.uptime(),
timestamp:newDate().toISOString(),
filesRootConfigured:peek.configured,
filesRootReady:Boolean(peek.exists&&peek.isDirectory),
});
log("GET",200,pathname,`${Date.now()-start}ms`);
return;
}

if(pathname==="/api/categories"){
constcategories=awaitfilesService.listCategories(PROJECT_ROOT);
sendJSON(res,200,categories);
log("GET",200,pathname,`${categories.length}categories·${Date.now()-start}ms`);
return;
}

if(pathname==="/api/releases"){
constlimit=url.searchParams.get("limit");
constreleases=awaitfilesService.listLatestReleases(PROJECT_ROOT,limit);
sendJSON(res,200,releases);
log("GET",200,pathname,`${releases.length}·${Date.now()-start}ms`);
return;
}

if(pathname==="/api/search"){
constq=url.searchParams.get("q")||"";
constlimit=url.searchParams.get("limit");
constdata=awaitfilesService.searchEntries(PROJECT_ROOT,q,{limit});
sendJSON(res,200,data);
log("GET",200,pathname,`${data.results.length}·${Date.now()-start}ms`);
return;
}

if(pathname==="/api/files"||pathname.startsWith("/api/files/")){
constrel=pathname==="/api/files"
?(url.searchParams.get("path")||"")
:relFromPrefix(pathname,"/api/files");
constentries=awaitfilesService.listPath(PROJECT_ROOT,rel);
sendJSON(res,200,entries);
log("GET",200,pathname,`${entries.length}items·${Date.now()-start}ms`);
return;
}

if(pathname==="/api/download"||pathname.startsWith("/api/download/")){
constrel=pathname==="/api/download"
?(url.searchParams.get("path")||"")
:relFromPrefix(pathname,"/api/download");
constfileInfo=awaitfilesService.getDownloadTarget(PROJECT_ROOT,rel);
streamDownload(req,res,fileInfo);
log(req.method,200,pathname,`${fileInfo.fileName}·${Date.now()-start}ms`);
return;
}

if(
pathname==="/"||
pathname==="/index.html"||
pathname==="/browse"||
pathname.startsWith("/browse/")
){
constext=path.extname(pathname).toLowerCase();
if(ext&&MIMES[ext]&&ext!==".html"){
serveStatic(pathname,res);
log("GET",200,pathname,`static·${Date.now()-start}ms`);
return;
}
serveSPA(res);
log("GET",200,pathname,`SPA·${Date.now()-start}ms`);
return;
}

serveStatic(pathname,res);
log("GET",200,pathname,`asset·${Date.now()-start}ms`);
}catch(err){
conststatus=err.httpStatus||500;
sendServiceError(res,err);
log(req.method,status,pathname,`${err.code||err.message}·${Date.now()-start}ms`);
}
});


⚠️ Breaking Change Warning This handler is the single point of coupling for the entire stack: every if block's pathname is a promise made to the frontend (see the fetch URLs in resource-service.js), to nginx.conf (proxy rules), and to the health checks. Reordering the API branches is safe (they are mutually exclusive exact matches), but reordering the method/blocklist gates below the API routing would let POSTs and /.env reach the API — a genuine security regression. Deleting the /__health alias breaks any monitor configured against it. And note the dual grammar of /api/files + /api/download: removing the segment form breaks the frontend outright (every URL is built by encodeRelPath/downloadHref), while removing the ?path= form breaks no current frontend code — but it is documented API surface (see the startup banner's /api/files/<category> style and any scripted/curl consumers), so deprecate it deliberately, never silently.

